{"entity": "openEuler", "type": "organization", "description": "openEuler is a Linux-based, open-source operating system project initiated by Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd. and now incubated and managed under the OpenAtom Foundation, established on December 31, 2019. It is a community-driven initiative designed to deliver a stable, secure, and high-performance platform for enterprise, cloud, server, edge, and embedded computing environments. The project emphasizes open governance, with transparent processes, dedicated Special Interest Groups (SIGs), and defined roles for committers and maintainers, supporting public releases, versioning, and community collaboration. openEuler is publicly governed and available for general use, with clear documentation, reproducibility, and broad ecosystem support.\n\nThe project supports multiple hardware architectures, including x86_64, aarch64, RISC-V, Kunpeng, Phytium, and MIPS, with strategic emphasis on RISC-V. This broad hardware compatibility enables deployment across diverse computing scenarios, including critical infrastructure in government, finance, transportation, communications, energy, and education sectors. It is distributed in long-term support (LTS) versions such as openEuler 20.03-LTS, 22.03-LTS, and 24.03-LTS, with SP1, SP2, SP3, and SP4 service packs offering extended security updates and stability. As a key migration target for legacy systems like CentOS 7.4, CentOS 8.2, and Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), it is supported by tools such as x2openEuler for upgrade and compatibility management.\n\nopenEuler serves as the foundation for Huawei’s EulerOS and other related offerings, evolving from a similar ecosystem but now distinctly positioned as a standalone, open-source project. It is used extensively in large-scale digital infrastructure, AI development, and embedded device deployments, with a dedicated embedded version and integration into systems such as the Shenzhou KunTai desktop computer, the D2000 platform for Atlas 200 driver installation, and as the OS foundation for databases like OpenGauss and secGear for confidential computing. The system supports advanced technologies including DevStation, Eggops, Gazelle networking, and 3D spatial computation tools.\n\nThe software ecosystem includes comprehensive tools such as SysMaster (system initialization), Euler-Copilot (developer assistant), oec-hardware for hardware compatibility testing, and open-eBackup. Official ISO images, software repositories, RPM-GPG-KEY for secure package verification, and mirrors via Huawei Cloud and Tsinghua University are available, with package management through DNF and YUM. Community-maintained repositories like COPR and oepkgs further expand the software landscape. The project is recognized internationally through platforms like DistroWatch and is a key participant in China’s national IT innovation (xinchuang) initiative.\n\nDespite its strengths, openEuler has faced challenges, including missing Docker packages by default, incompatibility with certain software (e.g., reptyr), SSH connection issues in VMs, and bugs affecting UDP transmission and GCC compilation. However, improvements in reproducibility have been implemented since 2022, and security is enhanced via features like encrypted GRUB2, dependency management, secure configuration benchmarks, and enforcement of policies such as PAM_wheel.so. The project continues to grow as a globally inclusive, architecture-agnostic, and innovation-driven open-source community.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project hosted by the OpenEuler Community.<SEP>openEuler is a Linux-based open-source operating system designed for enterprise use.<SEP>openEuler is an open-source operating system project that develops and maintains Linux distributions for enterprise and cloud environments."}
